# Paths the static bundle must never contain (#157).
#
# nginx serves the bundle BEFORE anything else: `try_files $uri $uri/index.html
# @fallback` in the catch-all means a file in the bundle shadows the route map
# or the Go service at the same address, silently and without a line in any
# log. That is the failure mode epic #142 names as the most likely way this
# seam goes wrong.
#
# A line ending in "/" is a prefix. A line containing "*" is a glob matched
# against the whole path. Anything else is an exact path. Blank lines and
# lines starting with "#" are ignored. Matching is done after stripping a
# locale prefix, so /ru/snapshots/x is checked as /snapshots/x.
#
# This list is not maintained by hand: service/deploytest derives what must be
# in it from places that know nothing about each other -- the Go routes, the
# route map in deploy/nginx/conf.d/openipc-redirects.conf, and every nginx
# location with an alias or a root of its own -- and fails if an entry is
# missing or if an entry here matches none of them.

# --- the application's own namespaces ---------------------------------------
/admin
/admin/
/api/
/rails/active_storage/
/up

# --- pages that are not pages -----------------------------------------------
/binaries
/telemetry
/telemetry/

# --- the gallery, which #165 extracts through a JSON API, not through here ---
# /open-wall and /open-wall/ were here until #165 extracted the gallery.
# /snapshots stays: cameras POST to it, and nginx's static handler answers
# POST too -- a file at that address would swallow every upload. The
# per-snapshot pages are served from the bundle's shell by a location of
# their own rather than by a file at their address, so the prefix below
# and the seam's catch-all both stay out of their way.
/snapshots
/snapshots/

# --- served from disk by nginx already, from a different tree ----------------
# /assets/ was the old asset pipeline's and answers 410 now. /fonts/ was here
# until #304 moved the faces into the bundle, which serves them itself.
/assets/
/images/
/wall/
/dl/
/files/
/.well-known/
# The Go firmware process's image cache (#299), internal: reached only through the X-Accel-Redirect the firmware role sends.
/firmware-cache/

# --- the expensive leaf ------------------------------------------------------
# /cameras/ is deliberately NOT reserved: the catalogue is exactly what #162
# extracts. This one action is, because it costs about a second of CPU and
# 8-32MB of disk per call and is guarded by two limit_req zones that a file
# served from the bundle would walk straight past.
*/download_full_image
